<!-- @after 7. Deep Dive: Chunked Resumable Download (15 min) :: animation -->
### Follow a download through a dropped network

<figure class="stepper" data-illustrated><span class="stepper-title">Three chunks, one interruption, no restart</span><div class="stage viz-stack"><div class="viz-grid" data-cols="3"><div class="node" data-el="caller" data-art="phone" data-art-role="client"><strong>App caller</strong><small data-text="callerV">enqueue(request)</small></div><div class="node" data-el="ledger" data-art="database" data-art-role="storage"><strong>Room ledger</strong><small data-text="ledgerV">empty</small></div><div class="node" data-el="worker" data-art="worker" data-art-role="queue"><strong>DownloadWorker</strong><small data-text="workerV">not started</small></div></div><div class="viz-grid" data-cols="3"><div class="node" data-el="server" data-art="server" data-art-role="runtime"><strong>File server</strong><small data-text="serverV">supports Range</small></div><div class="node" data-el="parts" data-art="document" data-art-role="storage"><strong>Part files</strong><small data-text="partsV">none</small></div><div class="node" data-el="final" data-art="document" data-art-role="storage"><strong>Final file</strong><small data-text="finalV">not present</small></div></div><div class="node is-key" data-el="event" data-art="signal" data-art-role="runtime"><strong>What happens</strong><small data-text="eventV">—</small></div></div><script type="application/json" class="steps">[
  {
    "label": "Enqueue is durable",
    "caption": "enqueue() inserts the download row in Room and then enqueues unique work named download-<id> with KEEP. Chunks aren’t planned yet, because the file’s size is still unknown. The queue entry now outlives the process.",
    "add": [
      "caller",
      "ledger",
      "worker",
      "server",
      "parts",
      "final",
      "event"
    ],
    "state": {
      "caller": "active",
      "ledger": "done"
    },
    "text": {
      "ledgerV": "1 download row",
      "eventV": "durable before any byte moves"
    },
    "handoff": {
      "from": "caller",
      "to": "ledger",
      "kind": "rows",
      "label": "download row"
    }
  },
  {
    "label": "HEAD, then plan once",
    "caption": "The worker sends a HEAD request to learn Content-Length and Accept-Ranges. It writes chunk rows only if none exist, so a re-run never re-plans. Here the file is 12 MB and is split into three 4 MB chunks (illustrative sizes). A server without range support gets one single chunk instead.",
    "state": {
      "caller": "done",
      "worker": "active",
      "server": "active"
    },
    "text": {
      "workerV": "planning",
      "serverV": "Accept-Ranges: bytes",
      "ledgerV": "chunks 0, 1, 2 PENDING",
      "eventV": "planning is one-time and idempotent"
    },
    "handoff": {
      "from": "worker",
      "to": "ledger",
      "kind": "rows",
      "label": "chunk rows"
    }
  },
  {
    "label": "Fetch ranges in parallel",
    "caption": "A semaphore lets up to three chunks run at once, each with its own Range request and part file. Bytes stream through Okio, so the file is never held in memory. A chunk is marked DONE only after it has been written in full.",
    "state": {
      "server": "done",
      "parts": "active"
    },
    "text": {
      "workerV": "3 chunks at once",
      "serverV": "206 Partial Content",
      "partsV": "part.0, part.1, part.2",
      "ledgerV": "chunk 0 DONE",
      "eventV": "DONE only after the whole chunk is written"
    },
    "handoff": {
      "from": "server",
      "to": "parts",
      "kind": "page",
      "label": "bytes"
    }
  },
  {
    "label": "The network drops",
    "caption": "Chunk 1 is cut off midway. The worker returns Result.retry() and keeps its partial file. If the process is killed instead, nothing is lost either: the ledger is in Room, and no download state lives only in memory.",
    "state": {
      "server": "error",
      "worker": "warn",
      "parts": "warn"
    },
    "text": {
      "serverV": "connection lost",
      "workerV": "Result.retry()",
      "partsV": "part.1 is partial",
      "eventV": "partial bytes stay on disk"
    }
  },
  {
    "label": "A later run fetches what’s missing",
    "caption": "The new run asks the ledger for chunks not marked DONE, so chunk 0 isn’t fetched again. Chunk 1 resumes from its start byte plus the part file’s length, using a new Range request.",
    "state": {
      "server": "done",
      "worker": "active",
      "parts": "active"
    },
    "text": {
      "serverV": "206 for the rest",
      "workerV": "notDoneFor(id)",
      "partsV": "part.1 growing",
      "eventV": "resume at a byte offset, not at zero"
    },
    "handoff": {
      "from": "ledger",
      "to": "worker",
      "kind": "rows",
      "label": "chunks not DONE"
    }
  },
  {
    "label": "When the server won’t cooperate",
    "caption": "HTTP lets a server ignore Range and answer 200 with the whole file. Those bytes start at byte 0, so they must never be appended to a partial chunk: the partial data is discarded and the download restarts as a single chunk. A 416 means the range is invalid, perhaps because the file’s size changed, so the download is re-planned.",
    "state": {
      "server": "warn",
      "parts": "warn"
    },
    "text": {
      "serverV": "200 or 416",
      "partsV": "partial data discarded",
      "workerV": "200: restart · 416: re-plan",
      "eventV": "never append a 200 to a partial chunk"
    }
  },
  {
    "label": "Assemble, rename, verify",
    "caption": "Assembly starts only after every chunk succeeds. The chunks are concatenated in order into a .tmp file and renamed, so the final name never holds a half-written file. If a checksum was supplied, a mismatch deletes the file and fails without retrying. A full disk also fails permanently and is shown to the user.",
    "state": {
      "server": "done",
      "worker": "done",
      "parts": "done",
      "final": "done",
      "event": "done"
    },
    "text": {
      "workerV": "markCompleted",
      "ledgerV": "COMPLETED",
      "finalV": "complete file",
      "eventV": ".tmp, rename, then Completed"
    },
    "handoff": {
      "from": "parts",
      "to": "final",
      "kind": "page",
      "label": "assembled"
    }
  }
]</script><figcaption>The Room ledger is the truth about which chunks are done. An interruption keeps the partial file, a later run fetches only what is missing, and the final name appears only after the chunks are assembled.</figcaption></figure>

<!-- @after 12. Further Reading :: recap -->
### Say these in the interview

- **The Room ledger is the truth, not the file.** On resume, any chunk not marked `DONE` is fetched again from its byte offset.
- **Check for Range support, and don’t rely on it.** `HEAD` looks for `Accept-Ranges: bytes` and falls back to one chunk if it’s absent. A server can still ignore a range and answer 200 from byte 0, so that response is never appended; the download restarts as one chunk.
- **Bound the concurrency.** Three chunks per download and two downloads at once are the defaults, with bytes streamed rather than buffered.
- **Only a finished file gets the final name.** Chunks are assembled into `.tmp` and renamed; a checksum mismatch deletes the file and fails without an automatic retry.
- **Retry only what can recover.** A network drop retries and keeps the partial file, a 416 re-plans, and a full disk fails permanently and is shown to the user.
- **WorkManager can stop a run at any time.** It stops a worker when constraints are lost, and at its 10-minute execution deadline, then retries the work later and ignores the stopped worker’s `Result`. The ledger is what lets that run continue. For a long download the user is watching, make it a long-running worker with `setForeground`.
